### One todo, created. The whole of "make a backend do something" from a 45-line descriptor.
# @name Create
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
## TEST-HAS-HEADER: Location
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/todos
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Buy milk {{runToken}}-crud",
  "status": "todo",
  "description": "Two litres, semi-skimmed.",
  "due_on": "2026-08-31"
}

### Read it back by the id the server assigned
# @name Read
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/todos/{{Create.response.body.$.id}}

### The same read, with the version the last one returned — nothing changed, so nothing is sent back.
### `audit: true` on the entity is the whole reason this is reachable: it is what mints `updated_at`,
### which is what an ETag is minted from.
# @name ReadUnchanged
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [304]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/todos/{{Create.response.body.$.id}}
If-None-Match: {{Read.response.headers.ETag}}

### Move it along. A PATCH is partial: `title`, `description` and `due_on` go unmentioned and stay.
# @name Update
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/todos/{{Create.response.body.$.id}}
If-Match: {{Read.response.headers.ETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "doing" }

### The tag the read handed out is now stale, and a stale precondition is refused rather than applied.
### This is the lost update `If-Match` exists to prevent: without the header the write below would
### land, and the caller would read the 200 as proof nobody else had touched the row.
# @name UpdateWithStaleTag
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [412]
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/todos/{{Create.response.body.$.id}}
If-Match: {{Read.response.headers.ETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "done" }

### The tag the accepted write returned is the one the next write must carry
# @name Finish
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/todos/{{Create.response.body.$.id}}
If-Match: {{Update.response.headers.ETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "done" }

### Gone
# @name Delete
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [204]
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/todos/{{Create.response.body.$.id}}

### And a read of a deleted row is 404 — the same 404 an id that never existed answers, deliberately:
### the entity declares no `softDelete`, so there is nothing left to distinguish.
# @name ReadDeleted
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/todos/{{Create.response.body.$.id}}
